# Line endings, pinned so a tarball built on Windows matches one built
# anywhere else.
#
# `R CMD build` converts C, C++ and Fortran sources and the makefiles under
# src/ to LF itself, and R CMD check verifies the shell scripts -- so those are
# safe either way. It does NOT touch .rs files or DESCRIPTION, which on a
# Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true shipped with CRLF. Rust's lexer and
# R's DCF parser both accept that, so nothing was broken, but there is no
# reason for the same commit to produce two different tarballs.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Sources and metadata that must not vary by platform.
*.rs         text eol=lf
*.toml       text eol=lf
*.R          text eol=lf
*.Rmd        text eol=lf
*.c          text eol=lf
*.h          text eol=lf
*.md         text eol=lf
DESCRIPTION  text eol=lf
NAMESPACE    text eol=lf

# Anything a Unix shell has to execute: a stray CR here is a hard failure
# ("$'\r': command not found"), not a cosmetic difference.
configure     text eol=lf
configure.win text eol=lf
cleanup       text eol=lf
cleanup.win   text eol=lf
*.sh          text eol=lf
src/Makevars*  text eol=lf

# Not text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.xz   binary
*.gz   binary
*.shp  binary
*.dbf  binary
*.shx  binary
